@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "4. Guide a character around a wall") {
        A character may know where it wants to go and still need help getting there. First steer toward a destination, then add a wall, inspect the blockage, and supply a route around it.

        This lesson uses an automatically moving character so you can focus on the difference between choosing movement and checking collisions.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-steering-04-preview.svg", alt: "A preview of the scene you will build in this lesson.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Choose a direction toward the destination") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            An ``Agent2D`` describes position, velocity, and movement limits. An ``AgentGoal`` calculates a steering force. The arrival goal slows the character as it gets near the destination instead of keeping its full speed.

            The sample's system applies that force, advances the agent, and places the visible player at the resulting position.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run stage one. The cyan square moves toward the green marker and slows near it. Find the arrival goal, the agent update, and the position assignment in the system.

                Restart route puts both the player and its velocity back at the start.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-steering-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-steering-01-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Change the slowing radius and watch when the player begins to slow down. Steering controls the desired motion; there is no wall yet to stop it.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Check whether the character can move there") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A steering goal does not prevent movement through walls. Add a ``CharacterControllerComponent`` to represent the player's physical outline and a ``CollisionComponent`` to represent the wall.

            Instead of assigning the desired position directly, ask `moveCharacter(by:)` to move the character. It checks the requested displacement and stops or slides where the character meets a collider.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage two. The player reaches the orange wall and cannot pass through it. Find the call to `moveCharacter(by:)`, then the code that copies the accepted position back to the agent.

                This system owns the character's movement. It does not also attach a dynamic physics body that would try to move the same entity.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-steering-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-steering-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-steering-02-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                The destination has not changed, yet the player cannot reach it. That is expected: a collision check can refuse a blocked move, but it cannot invent a route around the wall.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Ask what is in the way") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A raycast checks a line through the scene and reports the colliders it meets. It is useful for questions such as whether a wall blocks the direct line to a target.

            The sample excludes the player from its own query. The destination is only a visual marker, so the wall is the object the query reports.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage three and press Inspect direct line to destination. Read the result before the character reaches the wall and again after it stops.

                A ray has no width. Even if it misses a wall, your whole character may not fit. Keep the character controller responsible for the actual movement.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-steering-03.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-steering-02.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-steering-03-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Compare the question asked by the raycast with the question asked by the controller. One checks a line to the target. The other checks whether a shape can complete this frame's move.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Give the character a route") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            An ``AgentPath`` is a list of points to follow. This path goes above the fixed wall before turning toward the destination. The follow goal chooses motion along that route, while the controller still checks each move.

            For changing levels, a pathfinding graph can calculate those points. Here you supply them yourself so the roles remain easy to see.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use the final stage. The player travels above the wall and down toward the destination. Compare its path points with the wall's position.

                The route allows room for the character's radius. The path's own radius describes how closely to follow the route; it does not replace the collision shape.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-steering-04.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-steering-03.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-steering-04-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Restart and inspect the direct line early in the trip. It can still be blocked while the route around the wall works.

                You now have three separate tools: a goal chooses movement, a route supplies intermediate destinations, and a controller checks collisions. Continue with <doc:switching-levels-with-persistent-players> to organize a larger game.
            }
        }
    }
}
